# Talos single-node installation

Release 1.14.6 (2026-11-11), unchanged from the previous laboratory's image
selection. Only the previously verified ISO, signature bundles and client binary
are copied. The check machine itself is a new clean installation.

`verify.sh` validates the ISO and client SHA256 against the release checksum file
and their Sigstore bundles. The expected identity is the official repository's
`ci.yaml` workflow at `refs/tags/v1.14.6`, with the GitHub Actions OIDC issuer.
The verifier binary is pinned to its official release asset digest. The exact
Sigstore trust root is retained and pinned to an immutable upstream revision and
SHA256; see `raw/talos/trust-root-provenance.txt`. Evidence:
`raw/talos/verification.log`; the initial host launch used a relative path and
failed before verification; its corrected absolute-path launch succeeded.

`install.py` creates an empty 32 GiB virtio disk, generates the release client's
minimal control-plane configuration, and boots the official ISO in BIOS mode.
The generated `controlplane.yaml` and `talosconfig` are exact host-created inputs,
retained with the recipe; their bytes are never obtained from a check machine.
The install payload is pinned to the official factory image digest recorded in
`raw/talos/installer-digest.txt`; its image-factory signing identity and transparency
log inclusion were verified before the second installation. The first attempt used
the client default version tag and was stopped without acceptance; its exact inputs
and statuses are preserved under `attempt1/`. The install disk is fixed as `/dev/vda` from the QEMU drive definition, so no disk
query is performed. The node address comes from the fixed QEMU user network,
so no console or guest networking observation is needed.

A loopback forwarded API port provides instrument access. Talos does not use the
SSH key: its generated client certificate is the instrument credential. The
configuration adds the loopback certificate SAN needed by this transport. All
other generated defaults are retained. The configuration is applied once;
after a fixed settling interval, a graceful poweroff ends the ISO session,
then a disk-only boot is started. This also handles an installer that does not
automatically reboot. API-login command output is discarded. Bootstrap is invoked once,
then the system is shut down and the backing made read-only.

Only exit statuses of installation, API login and script execution are retained.
No health, service, object, disk, file, account or process listing is requested.
After registration, the planned disposable smoke test is `talosctl version`
with its entire output discarded followed by `exit 37`; expected status 37.
This validates authentication and the host-side `run` wrapper only.

Sources:

- https://github.com/siderolabs/talos/releases/tag/v1.14.6 — “Talos 1.14.6 (2026-11-11)”.
- https://docs.siderolabs.com/talos/v1.14/platform-specific-installations/bare-metal-platforms/iso — the ISO installs only after configuration is applied.
- https://docs.siderolabs.com/talos/v1.14/getting-started/getting-started — machine configuration generation, application and single bootstrap procedure.
